  "generated_text": " the the remainder when $p(x)$ is divided by $x - 3$ is 11, we have\n\\[ p(3) = 11 \\Rightarrow A(3)^5 + B(3)^3 + C(3) + 4 = 11 \\Rightarrow 243A + 27B + 3C = 7 \\]\n\nTo find the remainder when $p(x)$ is divided by $x + 3,$ we evaluate $p(-3)$:\n\\[ p(-3) = A(-3)^5 + B(-3)^3 + C(-3) + 4 = -243A - 27B - 3C + 4 \\]\n\nNotice that $-243A - 27B - 3C = -(243A + 27B + 3C) = -7.$ Therefore,\n\\[ p(-3) = -7 + 4 = -3 \\]\n\nThus, the remainder when $p(x)$ is divided by $x + 3$ is $\\boxed{-3}$.",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[-3, '-3']",
    "gold_answer": "By the Remainder Theorem, $p(3) = 11,$ so\n\\[A \\cdot 3^5 + B \\cdot 3^3 + C \\cdot 3 + 4 = 11.\\]Then $A \\cdot 3^5 + B \\cdot 3^3 + C \\cdot 3 = 7.$\n\nAgain by the Remainder Theorem, when $p(x)$ is divided by $x + 3,$ the remainder is\n\\begin{align*}\np(-3) &= A \\cdot (-3)^5 + B \\cdot (-3)^3 + C \\cdot (-3) + 4 \\\\\n&= -A \\cdot 3^5 - B \\cdot 3^3 - C \\cdot 3 + 4 \\\\\n&= -7 + 4 \\\\\n&= \\boxed{-3}.\n\\end{align*}",
    "is_correct": true
  },
